1 Welcome to Emacsvox
*********************

Emacsvox is a keyboard-driven spoken environment built on Emacs.  You
can use it to read and write text, browse the Web, manage mail and
messaging, work in shells, program, and explore structured information
through speech and sound.

   Emacsvox requires Emacs 30.2 or newer and is derived from Emacspeak.
Emacspeak's pioneering audio-desktop design and T.  V.  Raman's original
work remain the foundation of this project.

   Emacsvox works with Emacs commands and the structure of each
application.  It speaks the text you reach and announces relevant
changes, rather than reading a screenful of characters indiscriminately.
Distinctive voices mark structure; short sounds can identify an event
without adding a spoken label.

* Menu:

* Read the key instructions: Emacs Key Notation.  Learn which keys to hold together and which to press in sequence.
* Where to begin: Where To Begin.  Get speech working and choose a learning or reference route.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Emacs Key Notation,  Next: Where To Begin,  Up: Introduction

1.1 Read the key instructions
=============================

Emacs manuals use a compact notation for keyboard instructions.  Read
this section before trying the commands below.  You do not type the
notation as text: it tells you which keys to press.

1.1.1 Hold modifiers; release them between steps
------------------------------------------------

A _modifier_ is a key held while you press another key.  ‘C-’ means
Control, usually labelled Ctrl.  For ‘C-n’, hold Control, press
lowercase n, then release both keys.  The hyphen is part of the
notation, not a key to press.

   ‘M-’ means Meta, usually the Alt key.  Some macOS configurations use
Option or Command for Meta.  For ‘M-x’, hold the key your Emacs uses for
Meta, press x, and release both.  You can also press Escape, release it,
then press x; do not hold Escape.  This alternative is useful when Alt
is intercepted.

   ‘S-’ means Shift.  For ‘S-<TAB>’, hold Shift while pressing Tab.
‘C-M-f’ means hold Control and Meta together while pressing f.  When
several modifiers are joined by hyphens, hold them for that one
keypress.

1.1.2 Read spaces as separate steps
-----------------------------------

Spaces between keys separate successive keypresses.  They do not mean to
press the space bar.  For example, ‘C-x C-f’ means press Control-x,
release it, then press Control-f.  Keeping Control held for those two
letters also works, because both steps require it.

   The distinction matters in ‘C-e H’:

  1. Hold Control, press lowercase e, then release both keys.
  2. Hold Shift, press h to type capital H, then release both keys.

   Do not keep Control held for H.  The capital H is a letter, not the
name of another modifier.  Lowercase h and uppercase H are different
instructions.  With the Emacsvox defaults, ‘C-e h’ repeats Help, while
‘C-e H’ opens settings.  ‘C-h’ is different again: hold Control and
press h; it begins Emacs's Help commands.

   A longer route such as ‘C-e H v p’ starts the same way.  After
capital H, press lowercase v, then lowercase p, with no modifier held.
That route opens Voice Palettes when used from an ordinary working
buffer.  Letters offered inside a menu belong to that menu; typing p in
a document normally inserts p.

   A _prefix key_ starts an unfinished sequence.  After pressing one,
Emacs waits for the next key.  You may pause between steps; the notation
does not ask you to hold the whole sequence down at once.

1.1.3 Recognise named keys and command names
--------------------------------------------

‘<RET>’ means Return or Enter, ‘<TAB>’ means Tab, ‘<SPC>’ means the
space bar, and ‘<ESC>’ means Escape.  Press the named key, not the
letters of its name.  ‘<down>’ means the Down Arrow key; the angle
brackets are not typed.  "Shift-left" means hold Shift and press Left
Arrow.  Where this manual says "left/right", it offers either arrow, not
a slash to type.

   Some instructions run a command by name.  In ‘M-x describe-mode
<RET>’, press Meta-x, release it, type ‘describe-mode’ with its hyphen,
then press Enter.  The name is ordinary text entered at the command
prompt, unlike the modifier notation.  An instruction written ‘M-x
describe-mode’ also means to finish the name with Enter.

1.1.4 Cancel an unfinished instruction
--------------------------------------

‘C-g’ means hold Control and press g.  It normally cancels an unfinished
key sequence or prompt.  In Emacsvox's settings menus it goes back one
menu; ‘C-q’ closes the menu sequence.  These are cancellation and
navigation commands, not undo: changes already applied remain in effect.
An editor may ask what to do with unsaved work, so read that prompt
before answering.

   ‘C-u’ before another command supplies a _prefix argument_.  This is
different from an unfinished prefix key.  Some commands repeat; others
change their scope or behaviour.  Do not add it unless the instructions
call for it.

   Once speech works, ‘C-h k’ followed by a key sequence explains its
binding in the current buffer without invoking that command.  To check
the menu entry, enter ‘C-h k C-e H’: Control-h, lowercase k, Control-e,
then capital H.  Custom bindings and operating-system shortcuts can
affect what reaches Emacs.  For more detail, see *note Kinds of user
input: (emacs)User Input., *note Keys: (emacs)Keys., and *note Prefix
arguments: (emacs)Arguments. in the GNU Emacs Manual.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Where To Begin,  Prev: Emacs Key Notation,  Up: Introduction

1.2 Where To Begin
==================

Start with *note Installation: Installation. if Emacsvox is not yet
installed.  It explains the supported installation routes, takes you to
a clean speaking session, and provides recovery instructions when speech
does not start.  Once speech works, *note Learn the audio desktop:
(emacsvox)Guided Tutorial. teaches reading, editing, Help, structure,
and speech adjustments through six connected lessons and a disposable
document.  Return to *note Basic Usage: Basic Usage., *note Using Online
Help: Using Online Help., and *note Audio Desktop: Audio Desktop. as
everyday references.

   If Emacs itself is new to you, ‘C-h t’ opens its interactive
tutorial.  Emacsvox speaks the tutorial and your navigation while you
follow it.  At any point, ‘C-h m’ describes the active modes and ‘C-h k’
followed by a key explains its current command.

   *note Using this manual: Using This Manual. offers separate reading
routes for new and experienced Emacs users, with a short exercise in
finding a topic and returning to work.  For one question, *note Find a
task: Finding Tasks. links everyday goals to their instructions.  Use
*note Terms used in this manual: Emacsvox Terminology. when a name such
as palette or profile is unfamiliar.  Detailed backend configuration
comes later; it is not a prerequisite for everyday use once speech
works.

1.3 The Welcome Screen
======================

At an idle startup, after speech is ready, Emacsvox offers a short
welcome screen.  Its actions test speech, adjust rate and output
volumes, choose voices and engines, open the tutorial or manual, and
start normal use.  Move between actions with ‘<TAB>’ and ‘S-<TAB>’,
activate one with ‘<RET>’, and use ‘q’ to return.  Arrow keys read the
instructions normally.

   Toggle _Show this welcome screen at startup_ to stop showing it
automatically.  The choice is saved in this Emacs profile and survives
application upgrades.  Open the screen again through Help's _Emacsvox
welcome_ entry, ‘C-h W’, or ‘M-x emacsvox-welcome’.  Startup leaves
explicitly opened files and sessions already in use alone.

1.4 What You Will Hear
======================

Emacsvox combines several forms of feedback.  Speech reads text,
prompts, results, and status.  Voice changes make syntax and document
structure audible.  Short auditory icons and tones identify events such
as opening a file or completing an action.  Pauses and spatial cues help
separate related information.

   Begin with the supplied settings and adjust them as you learn what
helps your work.  From the buffer you are using, ‘C-e H’ opens the
Emacsvox settings menu.  Press ‘s’ for speech controls, ‘v’ for voices
and engines, or ‘o’ for sounds and output.  ‘C-e S’ opens focused
settings for the current integration, falling back to general buffer
settings when no focused menu is available.  To understand a particular
item's feedback, leave point on it and press ‘C-e E’.

   In a settings menu, up/down selects an item, left/right changes
sections, and ‘<RET>’ activates the selection.  ‘?’ followed by an
item's key explains that item; ‘? ?’ explains the menu.  ‘q’ returns one
level and ‘C-q’ closes the menu sequence.  Leaving a menu does not
reverse adjustments already applied.  Settings and editors have distinct
saving rules, explained alongside each task in *note Aural Presentation:
Aural Presentation.

   Use ‘C-e H a’ to find an action, or ‘C-e H /’ to find a setting.
Both select the matching menu control before you activate it.  Dedicated
editors retain their own navigation, preview, and saving commands; ‘?’
explains each view.

   Emacsvox sends speech through a speech server.  New GNU/Linux, macOS,
and WSL2 installations use Omnivox; established direct servers remain
available for compatible existing setups.  Installation introduces what
you need for first speech.  *note Speech Backends: Speech Backends.
provides the platform and compatibility details.

1.5 About This Manual
=====================

The maintained chapters teach current setup and everyday tasks.  Each
explains what you do, what feedback to expect, and how to inspect or
recover the result.  The separate generated reference records commands,
options, and keymaps from the source.  The heritage manual preserves
Emacspeak essays and application guides for their historical and design
value; it is not a current setup guide.

   Rapidly changing features are also documented in ‘README.org’,
‘etc/NEWS’, and the generated Aural Presentation reference.  When a
copied binding differs from your running Emacs, consult ‘C-h k’, ‘C-h
m’, or ‘C-h b’ for the commands and bindings available in that session.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Using This Manual,  Next: Installation,  Prev: Introduction,  Up: Top

2 Using this manual
*******************

You can read the early chapters in sequence or use this manual to answer
a question while working.  *note Find a task: Finding Tasks. links
everyday goals to their detailed instructions.  *note Terms used in this
manual: Emacsvox Terminology. explains names such as palette, profile,
scope, and draft without requiring you to study the whole settings
system first.

   Start with *note Read the key instructions: Emacs Key Notation. if
Emacs notation is unfamiliar.  It explains which keys to hold together,
when to release them, and how to enter a command by name.  The reading
routes below assume that notation, not prior experience with Emacs.

2.1 Choose a reading route
==========================

After *note Installation: (emacsvox)Installation. gives you speech, work
through *note Learn the audio desktop: (emacsvox)Guided Tutorial. for
six progressive lessons using a disposable practice document.  It
teaches reading, editing, Help, structure, temporary settings, and voice
previews, with exercises and separate answers.  No additional engine is
needed.

   If Emacs itself is new to you, ‘C-h t’ opens its separate interactive
tutorial.  Use it for more practice with general editing.  *note Basic
Usage: (emacsvox)Basic Usage., *note Using Online Help: (emacsvox)Using
Online Help., and *note Audio Desktop: (emacsvox)Audio Desktop. are
references to return to as you work.

   If you already use Emacs, start with *note Basic Usage:
(emacsvox)Basic Usage. once speech works.  Keep your usual editing and
mode commands.  The tutorial's *note temporary-settings lesson:
(emacsvox)Tutorial Temporary Settings. and *note voice experiments:
(emacsvox)Tutorial Voice Experiments. provide guided practice without
making its earlier editing lessons prerequisites for experienced users;
prepare its disposable buffer before experimenting.  Then read *note
Aural Presentation: (emacsvox)Aural Presentation. for application
meaning, voices, sounds, and settings, and choose an application guide
for your work.

   For one question, go directly to *note Find a task: Finding Tasks.,
use Info's index, or search the manual text.  When something is not
working, *note Something is wrong: Finding Problem Tasks. starts with
symptoms rather than the names of settings.  *note Speech Backends:
(emacsvox)Speech Backends. contains alternative installation and backend
configuration procedures; it is not required reading between your first
speaking session and everyday editing.  The final Developer Guide is for
changing and maintaining Emacsvox, not for ordinary use.

2.2 Find an answer in Info and return
=====================================

Info is Emacs's manual reader.  A _node_ is one navigable topic, which
can have its own menu of subtopics.  These instructions apply to the
Info buffer, not to Emacsvox's settings menus.

   Try this lookup without changing your configuration:

  1. From a working buffer, press ‘C-h i’ to open Info, ‘d’ for its
     directory, then ‘m’.  Enter ‘Emacsvox’ and press ‘<RET>’ to open
     this manual.
  2. Press ‘i’, enter ‘ordinary reading voice’, and press ‘<RET>’.  The
     index takes you to the voice-task links.  Read with your ordinary
     speech commands.
  3. Use ‘<TAB>’ to reach _Change my ordinary reading voice_ and press
     ‘<RET>’.  You are now at the named-voice instructions.  Reading
     them changes nothing; leave the editing commands for when you
     deliberately want to make a change.
  4. Press lowercase ‘l’ to return to the task links.  Another ‘l’
     returns to the topic you were reading before the index lookup.
     This follows your visiting history rather than the chapter order.
  5. Press ‘q’ to leave Info.  From an Aural interface's contextual
     manual link, ‘q’ returns to that interface.  If you opened other
     windows or switched buffers during the exercise, ‘C-x b’ also lets
     you select your original working buffer by name.

   If the directory has no Emacsvox entry, the manual is not yet
discoverable by that Emacs session; follow *note Installation:
(emacsvox)Installation. rather than guessing a file path.  In an Aural
editor, ‘C-c C-i’ opens its relevant manual topic, and ‘i’ in that
editor's Help opens the same topic.

   The following Info commands are useful during later lookups:

‘i’
     Look up a subject in the manual's indexes.  Use ordinary words, a
     command name, or a key sequence.  ‘,’ visits further matching
     entries.
‘s’
     Search the manual text, including other nodes.  Enter the words and
     press ‘<RET>’; ‘s <RET>’ continues the same search.
‘TAB, S-TAB’
     Move among links.  ‘<RET>’ follows the selected link.
‘m’
     Choose a subtopic from the current node's menu by name, with
     completion.
‘u’
     Go to the containing topic.  This is different from ‘l’, which goes
     back to the topic you last visited.
‘g’
     Go to a node by its identifier.  For example, enter ‘Finding Tasks’
     to return directly to the task finder.

   For the full reader tutorial and search behaviour, see the *note Info
manual: (info)Top.  If a key behaves differently in your session, ‘C-h
k’ followed by that key explains its effective binding without invoking
it.

2.3 Recognise the document you are reading
==========================================

This user manual explains tasks, feedback, and recovery.  The generated
_Emacsvox Reference Manual_ lists commands, options, and keymaps from
source.  The heritage manual preserves historical Emacspeak material; it
is not a current setup guide.  A linked application's own manual remains
authoritative for that application's general operations.

   Some stable Info identifiers predate their displayed headings.  For
example, ‘Aural Home’ now identifies _Getting started with settings
menus_.  This keeps old links and bookmarks valid; it does not make the
retired Home menu entry part of the learning route.

   In HTML, use the contents, headings, and task links, then your
browser's Back command to return from a link.  The Info keys above are
not browser commands.  The published website may describe a different
revision from your installed manual.  Use the manual matching your
Emacsvox version, and check live Help when a key differs from the
documented default.


